{"text": "\\) G LAND RIGHTS LETTER\n\na D.O. Box 568, Sharpaburg, Maryland 21782\ny Phone & fax: 301-797-745\n\nJuly 1, 1993\n\nDear Readers, .\nWith all good things there comes a time to make them better...\n\nIhave finally come to the conclusion that, for the sake of my family and farm, Ican\u2019t continue\nto publish Land Rights Letter from my living room! In addition, Land Rights Letter needs\nto be put on sounder financial footing, which I can\u2019t work toward while writing and editing for\na deadline each month. There simply aren\u2019t enough hours in the day. Therefore, as a result of\nLRL\u2019s success and acceptance, it\u2019s time to make improvements. Unfortunately, because of this\nneed to reorganize and improve Land Rights Letter and make its production more profes-\nsional, we won't be sending a July issue. - End\nWith the help of some dedicated new board members who are committed to not only keeping\nLRL alive but to expanding its mission, Land Rights Letter will be back with an August issue\nand some exciting changes:\n\n\u00a2 The new board is committed to expanding the readership by reducing the subscription\nrate to $20 for twelve issues. Of course, the success of the new subscription price depends\non adding many new readers and finding additional funding sources. Please let us know\nif you have ideas for other sources of funds and consider making a charitable contribu-\ntion yourself.\n\n\u00a2 To streamline subscriber services and to respond to the many phone inquiries LRL\nreceives, all administrative functions will be handled by a new office in New York. The\nMaryland office will serve only as the editorial office. Please address general correspon-\ndence to: Land Rights Letter, P.O. Box 1111, Gloversville, NY 12078. Call 518-725-8835.\nContinue to send story ideas to the Maryland address.\n\n\u00a2 And the best news of all is that the new board plans to enhance Land Rights Letter\u2019s\neducational function by building a library, an archives if you will, of property rights\ninformation for its readers. 1 have been informally acting as a clearinghouse, but the\ndemands for that service have become so great that the board is determined to raise\nfunds for a more professional approach to providing readers with educational services.\n\nHelp us find new readers! The reduced subscription rate is dependent on increasing our\nreadership. We hope to make the slash in price permanent, so please use the envelope enclosed\nto add to your subscription, buy a gift for a friend, a public official, or a member of the media,\nand make a charitable contribution if you can. And, as always, your input is encouraged. Please\nwrite to our new office in New York with your ideas and suggestions.\nAnd, finally, have patience with us as we make this necessary transition. Alone I could never\nmake Land Rights Letter the educational resource it has the potential to be. However, with\nsome dedicated and enthusiastic help from a new group of board members, I think LRL has a\nvery bright future. We thank you in advance for your continued loyalty and support.\n\nesincerely,\n\nAnn G. Corcoran\n\u2019 Editor\n\nP.S. Your subscription may have expired! Please check the date on your mailing label. For\nexample, a 0693 after your name means that your subscription expired with the June 1993 issue\nof LRL. We will of course add a month to all other subscriptions to make up for the July issue.",
